    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Summary\n\n`respawn-pane` and the pane-pid probe hardcode the tmux target `<session>:0.0`, and nothing in the runtime reads tmux's `base-index` / `pane-base-index`. Both default to `0`, but setting them to `1` is one of the most common tmux customisations there is \u2014 so on those machines the session's only pane is `<session>:1.1` and every `<session>:0.0` target misses.\n\nThe user-visible effect is that **`Runtime.Restart` always fails**, which is the path session restore uses. It also fails `TestRuntimeIntegrationSupervisedExitKeepsInteractiveShell` and `TestAttachmentStreamsRealTmuxPane`, so the failure looks like flaky integration tests rather than a runtime bug.\n\nTwo sites, both in `backend/internal/adapters/runtime/tmux/commands.go`:\n\n```go\n// :24  respawnPaneArgs\n\"respawn-pane\", \"-k\",\n\"-t\", id + \":0.0\",\n\n// :61  panePIDArgs\nreturn []string{\"display-message\", \"-p\", \"-t\", id + \":0.0\", \"#{pane_pid}\"}\n```\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nSelf-contained, no AO required \u2014 it isolates its own server and config:\n\n```sh\nCONF=$(mktemp); printf 'set -g base-index 1\\nset -gw pane-base-index 1\\n' > \"$CONF\"\nexport TMUX_TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d)\ntmux -f \"$CONF\" new-session -d -s aorepro 'sleep 300'\n\ntmux list-panes -a -F '#{session_name}:#{window_index}.#{pane_index}'   # aorepro:1.1\n\ntmux respawn-pane -k -t aorepro:0.0 -c /tmp sh -c 'sleep 300'   # what AO does today\ntmux respawn-pane -k -t aorepro   -c /tmp sh -c 'sleep 300'     # proposed\n```\n\nAgainst AO itself, with `base-index 1` / `pane-base-index 1` in your tmux config:\n\n```\ncd backend && go test ./internal/adapters/runtime/tmux/ -run TestRuntimeIntegrationSupervisedExitKeepsInteractiveShell\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`Restart` respawns the session's only pane regardless of the user's `base-index` / `pane-base-index`.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n```\ntmux runtime: restart session <id>: exit status 1: can't find pane: 0\n```\n\nThe exact message depends on which option is non-zero, because tmux resolves the window before the pane. Measured on tmux 3.7c, single AO-style session, `respawn-pane -k -t <s>:0.0`:\n\n| `base-index` | `pane-base-index` | real target | result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0 | `c:0.0` | ok |\n| 0 | 1 | `c:0.1` | `can't find pane: 0` |\n| 1 | 0 | `c:1.0` | `can't find window: 0` |\n| 1 | 1 | `c:1.1` | `can't find window: 0` |\n\nOne wrinkle worth flagging: **`panePIDArgs` does not fail.** `display-message -p -t <s>:0.0 '#{pane_pid}'` silently falls back rather than erroring, and in every single-pane case above it returned the correct pid. So that call site is latent today rather than broken \u2014 but it is the same unchecked assumption, and it would stop being harmless the moment a session has more than one pane.\n\n### AO version\n\n`ao version dev`, built from `main` at `759595a9`.\n\n### Environment\n\n- Arch Linux, tmux 3.7c\n- `base-index 1`, `pane-base-index 1` set globally in `~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf`\n- Reproduced against `main` with no local patches; the standalone snippet above reproduces with no AO at all\n\n### Logs / extra context\n\n**Suggested fix \u2014 target the session name alone.** `respawn-pane -t <session>` and `display-message -t <session>` resolve to the session's current window and active pane, which for AO is the only pane (`respawnPaneArgs`' own comment says \"the session's only pane\", and there is no `new-window` or `split-window` anywhere in the runtime). Verified working on the `base-index 1` / `pane-base-index 1` server above.\n\nThat also matches what this file already does elsewhere \u2014 `setStatusOffArgs`, `setMouseOnArgs` and `setWindowSizeLargestArgs` all pass bare `id`, with a comment explaining the targeting choice. So the fix makes the file more internally consistent, not less.\n\nIf you would rather keep an explicit pane target, the index-safe form is to resolve it once (`display-message -p -t <session> '#{pane_id}'` gives a stable `%N` that is immune to both options) and target that. I have a preference for the simpler one but no strong opinion \u2014 happy to send whichever you prefer as a PR, with the two integration tests as the regression proof.\n\nI did not fold this into #4654 or #4655 (OneDev provider work) since it is unrelated to those.\n",
